flintc build: compile to a native executable

Usage:
  flintc build [options] file.fl

  The executable is named after the file (hello.fl makes ./hello); -o writes it
  somewhere else. --test builds the program that 'flintc test' would run.

Options:
  -O0, -O2             no optimization, with debugging aids (default); or optimized
  --overflow=<mode>    integer overflow: trap (default) or wrap
  -o, --output <path>  where to write the executable
  --color=<when>       colored output: auto (default), always or never
  -h, --help           show this help

  Bounds, null, cast and division checks are always on: only 'unsafe' removes them.

For a project (what 'flint' passes):
  --pkg <name>=<root.fl>       a package that can be imported, and its root file
  --dep [<pkg>:]<as>=<name>    package <pkg> (without it: the program) imports <name> as <as>
  --include [<pkg>:]<dir>      where the C headers that <pkg> (or the program) imports are
  --object <file.o>            an object to link with the program
  --lib <name>                 a library to link with it (-l<name>)
  --cache <dir>                keep what's built there, and redo only what changed
  --test                       build the program that runs the `test` blocks
  --all-files                  the tests of every .fl file under the file's directory too

Examples:
  flintc build hello.fl
  flintc build -O2 hello.fl -o bin/hello
